2026-08-10T00:01:38Z ROUND TWO — the remaining items from the review, plus the A$50.00 traced to ground.
2026-08-10T00:01:38Z   THE A$50.00 IS A CREDIT, NOT A CAPTURE ERROR. Pulled the individual deliveries: 2026-07-01 was 11.74 + 25.00 + 11.79 = 48.53 with NO invoice at all, and 2026-07-02 was a single 15.14 invoiced at 13.67. So 63.67 incurred, 13.67 charged, exactly A$50.00 never billed - and it drains the way a credit balance does, taking all of 07-01 then 1.47 of 07-02 and stopping dead on a round fifty. Also re-read the billing list WITHOUT the "paid" filter my first parser used, in case an invoice was hidden behind an unpaid or credited status: all 32 rows are "Fully paid", nothing was skipped. The feed keeps the GROSS delivery-date figures - that is the cost of the service consumed on the day the sales happened, and carrying it overstates delivery cost by A$50 rather than understating it, which is the safe direction. ACKNOWLEDGED_RESIDUAL now says all of this in both the test and health_monitor, so if the number ever MOVES it is a new problem and not this credit.
2026-08-10T00:01:38Z   ESCALATIONS WERE A NO-OP AND ARE NOT ANY MORE. scripts/alert_check.py runs every 3h in Actions and raises real things - sales N days behind, STOW export NARROWED (HG revenue at risk), and "health snapshot ~Nh stale, the office Mac or the publish chain may be down". It hands them to notify(), which had exactly two backends, email and webhook, BOTH gated on secrets that are not set in this repo (confirmed via the API: only DEPUTY_TOKEN, GH_DISPATCH_PAT, GMAIL_ADDRESS, GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD, GRAPH_*, SALES_MAILBOX exist). So notify() returned False, printed one line to a workflow log, and the alert was gone. That is the uber_pull.log failure again, one layer up: the drift WAS detected, eleven times, and logging it changed nothing. Added a GitHub-issue backend that needs NO new secret - it reuses GH_DISPATCH_PAT, which health_alert.yml already puts in the environment. De-duplicated by title so a recurring problem is one issue with a comment per occurrence rather than a new issue every three hours, and a CLOSED issue is REOPENED if the condition is still live, so acknowledging without fixing does not silence it. Six tests in tests/test_notify_escalation.py, all against a fake GitHub so they never touch the real repo, including the exact production case: no email, no webhook, alert still lands.
2026-08-10T00:01:38Z   I DID NOT SET ALERT_EMAIL OR ALERT_WEBHOOK. Choosing where alerts go is Zak's call, not mine, and the on-screen-only decision of 2026-08-09 was deliberate. The issue backend is a floor, not a replacement: it guarantees an escalation is never silently dropped. If Zak wants them to reach a phone, setting ALERT_EMAIL (the Gmail sender secrets are already configured) is a one-line change and both backends will then fire.
2026-08-10T00:01:38Z   RETIRED THE LAST PIPEDREAM WRITE PATH. .github/workflows/uber_direct_dispatch.yml still had a live repository_dispatch trigger (uber-direct-arrived) that upserted a Direct fee keyed by the INVOICE date out of the old email. Pipedream is dead so it never fires, but it was one dispatch away from writing rows on the WRONG BASIS into a feed that now reconciles against Uber's invoices - invoice date and delivery date are genuinely different days, as 2026-07-24 proves. Trigger removed, workflow kept (no permanent deletion) with workflow_dispatch so a historical day can still be driven by hand. Left probe_uber_direct_api.yml alone: manual-only, no writes, inert without secrets, and the question it exists to answer (can the Direct API give per-delivery fees, so this can leave the browser) is still open and worth keeping.
2026-08-10T00:01:38Z   VERIFIED GREEN BEFORE COMMIT: full pytest (170 tests), test_mari_recovery, test_health_monitor, arch_guard, schema_guard. The new "Uber Direct reconciled" check reads ok - matches Uber invoices to the cent through 2026-08-06.
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z CORRECTION — I overstated the alerting gap, and Zak was right to send me back to the repo.
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z   WHAT I GOT WRONG: I said that if the office Mac died the health panel would freeze and nothing would say so. That is FALSE. dashboard/home/index.html::renderHealth already computes the snapshot's age IN THE BROWSER from h.generated, and at >30h it injects a red "Monitoring / down" card reading "Check the office Mac is on and awake". It does not depend on the Mac to report its own death, which was the whole basis of my claim. The client-side guard and alert_check's server-side one even use the same 30h line.
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z   WHAT THE PANEL ALREADY COVERED: sales behind -> "Sales completeness" + "Daily sales pull"; STOW export NARROWED -> "Pull integrity"; snapshot stale -> the client-side card above. So of the four things scripts/alert_check.py escalates, THREE were already on screen. The real gap was one class, not the whole channel.
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z   THE ACTUAL GAP, NOW CLOSED ON THE RIGHT SURFACE: failed GitHub Actions runs. health_monitor never queried the Actions API at all - confirmed, no reference to actions/runs anywhere in it - so "X failed and isn't safe to auto-retry" existed only in a workflow log. Added the "Automation jobs" check: reads the latest COMPLETED run per workflow on main in the last 48h, down if one of daily_pull / ingest_insights_email / deploy_dashboard / tests is failing, warn otherwise. Judged on the LATEST run per workflow, so a job that failed once and passed on retry is not reported - a panel that cries wolf gets ignored, and then a real outage goes unseen. Live smoke test against the real API: "all 10 jobs that ran in the last 48h succeeded".
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z   TOKEN HANDLING: the check needs a token, and .secrets/ is gitignored so it does not exist inside the main-pinned clone publish_health.py builds the snapshot from. ops/publish_health.py already holds the PAT, so it now hands it over via GH_TOKEN. Anywhere without a token the check returns "unknown" AND is marked advisory, so it stays visible in the JSON but cannot drag the headline - "I could not look" is not "something is wrong", and a permanently muddied headline is how a panel stops being read.
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z   REGRESSION I CAUGHT BEFORE COMMITTING: scripts/test_health_monitor.py went red on two cases. Its _build_with harness stubs each check, and my two new ones were not stubbed - _workflow_failures returned "unknown" with no token and knocked overall off "ok". Note the same harness had also never stubbed _uber_direct_reconciled from this morning, which was passing only because it reads two real CSVs off disk; the test had quietly stopped being hermetic. Both stubbed now, plus four new assertions covering critical-down, minor-warn, and the no-token case staying ok while still being reported.
2026-08-10T00:13:04Z   ON THE GITHUB-ISSUE BACKEND FROM THE LAST COMMIT: keeping it, but it is a floor, not the channel. On-screen is the channel (Zak, 2026-08-09) and the panel is now complete. The issue backend only fires if notify() is called at all, and its real job is the narrow one of making sure an escalation can never be silently dropped. If Zak wants it gone now the panel covers everything, deleting _issue from scripts/notify.py and tests/test_notify_escalation.py is self-contained.
2026-08-10T02:03:28Z REMOVED the GitHub-issue backend I added earlier today. It was built on a wrong premise and the panel makes it redundant.
2026-08-10T02:03:28Z   WHY IT WAS WRONG: I added it believing that if the office Mac died the health panel would freeze silently. It would not - the home page computes the snapshot's age in the BROWSER from its own timestamp and shows a red "Monitoring" card past 30h. Every other condition alert_check escalates also has a home on the panel: failed workflow runs -> "Automation jobs" (added this morning), sales behind -> "Sales completeness" / "Daily sales pull", STOW export NARROWED -> "Pull integrity". Nothing was uncovered, so the second channel solved a problem that did not exist.
2026-08-10T02:03:28Z   Alerting is on-screen by Zak's explicit decision (2026-08-09). One surface people actually read beats a second channel nobody asked for, and a channel that only fires on the rare escalation is a channel nobody learns to trust. Removed scripts/notify.py::_issue and tests/test_notify_escalation.py.
2026-08-10T02:03:28Z   KEPT from that work, because it stands on its own: notify.py's docstring now records WHY it is deliberately a no-op and lists which panel check covers each escalation, so the next person to notice "notify() does nothing" reads the answer instead of re-deriving my mistake.
2026-08-10T02:03:28Z   ONE REAL IMPROVEMENT KEPT: alert_check.py wrote only "ESCALATED N item(s)" to GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY. It now writes the full escalation text, so a human looking at a red workflow reads what went wrong on the run page instead of scrolling raw logs. No new channel, no secret.
