2026-08-16T23:25:10Z   A NEW PAGINATION TRAP, AND THE ONE REAL CATCH OF THIS RUN. The invoice walk was first done as two javascript_tool calls to stay under the 45s CDP timeout, and the SECOND call clicked Next before grabbing. Because the first call had already advanced past the page it last read, PAGE 5 WAS NEVER PARSED: the walk reported a tidy 7 pages, 31 invoices, Next correctly disabled at the end, and every structural check passed. It looked exactly like a complete walk. It was caught ONLY by the count - 31 parsed against 36 stored - and the five missing invoices (07-02, 07-04, 07-05, 07-08, 07-10, totalling A$253.73) are precisely one page. Had this been a first-ever run with nothing stored to compare against, a A$253.73 hole would have gone in silently and the reconciliation would have been quietly wrong by that amount. THE RULE: when a paginated walk is split across calls, GRAB THE CURRENT PAGE FIRST AND ADVANCE LAST, and carry the "have I already grabbed this page" state in the window global alongside the results - do not infer it from where the cursor happens to sit. The walk was redone from page 1 grab-then-advance and returned the full 8 pages / 36 invoices. Note this is the same shape of failure as the 50-row truncation and the zero-row window: a structurally valid result that is simply too small.
