2026-08-22T02:02:00Z   A /tmp COLLISION TRAP FIRED AT THE START OF THIS RUN AND IS WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE IT ALMOST SEEDED THE WHOLE RUN WITH YESTERDAY'S DATA. The runbook warns about root-owned /tmp payload files; the same hazard applies to the INPUT side. /tmp/uberrun still existed from the 2026-08-21 run, owned by nobody:nogroup, and `curl -o /tmp/uberrun/uber_daily.csv` FAILED WITH "Failure writing output to destination" while still reporting HTTP 200 - and the stale file sat there ready to be read as if it were origin/main. It was caught only because all three files reported an identical 1369-byte download, which is impossible for three files of different sizes. Fix applied: every run should `mktemp -d` its own scratch dir for BOTH inputs and payloads, never a fixed path. This run used /tmp/uber.WhAKs5PM and re-fetched all three CSVs plus the log into it.
